{"step_index":1193,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:27:33Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:27:33+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:27:33+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n\"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:27:16+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:27:17+02:00\\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_weight.lean`\\nTotal Lines: 42\\nTotal Bytes: 1343\\nShowing lines 1 to 42\\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\\n1: import Mathlib\\n2: import LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\\n3: \\n4: open SimpleGraph\\n5: open GraphCalc\\n6: \\n7: lemma two_le_romanWeight {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\\n8:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\\n9:     (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) (f : V → Fin 3)\\n10:     (hf : GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) :\\n11:     2 ≤ GraphCalc.romanWeight f := by\\n12:   unfold GraphCalc.romanWeight\\n13:   unfold GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass at hnt\\n14:   unfold GraphCalc.order at hnt\\n15:   by_contra hc\\n16:   have h_le1 : ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≤ 1 := by omega\\n17:   have h_no2 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 2 := by\\n18:     intro v hv\\n19:     have h_mem := Finset.single_le_sum (fun u _ => Nat.zero_le (f u : ℕ)) (Finset.mem_univ v)\\n20:     rw [hv] at h_mem\\n21:     omega\\n22:   have h_no0 : ∀ v : V, (f v : ℕ) ≠ 0 := by\\n23:     intro v hv\\n24:     have h_f0 : f v = 0 := by\\n25:       ext\\n26:       exact hv\\n27:     obtain ⟨u, _, hu2⟩ := hf v h_f0\\n28:     have hu2_nat : (f u : ℕ) = 2 := by\\n29:       rw [hu2]\\n30:       rfl\\n31:     exact h_no2 u hu2_nat\\n32:   have h_ge1 : ∀ v : V, 1 ≤ (f v : ℕ) := by\\n33:     intro v\\n34:     have h0 := h_no0 v\\n35:     omega\\n36:   have h_sum_ge : ∑ v : V, 1 ≤ ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) :=\\n37:     Finset.sum_le_sum (fun v _ => h_ge1 v)\\n38:   rw [Finset.sum_const, nsmul_eq_mul, mul_one] at h_sum_ge\\n39:   rw [Finset.card_univ] at h_sum_ge\\n40:   have h2 : 2 ≤ ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ) := hnt.trans h_sum_ge\\n41:   omega\\n42: \\nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\\n\"\r\n\"**Investigating Roman Domination Number**\\n\\nAnalyzing the worker's proven statements, specifically the lower bound on the Roman weight, to explore the relationship between the Roman domination number and graph radius.  The question is if a Roman weight of at least 2 implies a Roman domination number of at least 2.\\n\\nConsidering a hypothetical case where `2 ≤ romanDominationNumber G`, and exploring a scenario where `radius G ≤ 1`.  If so, the radius plus one is also less than or equal to 2, which itself is less than or equal to the domination number.\\n\\n\\n**Exploring Relationship Between Radius and Roman Domination**\\n\\nConsidering the implications of `radius G ≥ 2` and `romanDominationNumber G < Fintype.card V`. Investigating whether `radius G + 1 ≤ romanDominationNumber G` can be proven universally or if a counterexample exists. Examining a test theorem, suspecting its generality concerning any Roman dominating function.\\n\\nThis suggests that non-optimal Roman dominating functions might make the inequality easier to satisfy, due to higher weight. Considering a graph with radius 3 and a Roman dominating function of weight 3; the theorem would imply radius+1 (4) less than or equal to roman weight (3), which is false. Checking previous proof and, if the roman domination number is 3, the radius is at most 2.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Roman Domination Number**\\n\\nConsidering the implications of a Roman dominating function where vertices with positive values bound the graph's radius. The previous work proving `radius_add_one_le_card` provides a key insight. Realizing that the worker stopped at `test_branch.lean` because formalizing that the radius is bounded by the vertices with non-zero values requires explicit proof.\\n\\nThe core idea is to formalize the constraint: if a vertex is not in the ball of a given radius around a vertex with positive value, it must be dominated (or protected) by another Roman vertex. Attempting to define `romanDominationNumber G`.\\n\\n\\n**Refining Roman Domination Number Analysis**\\n\\nAnalyzing if `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f` holds for all Roman dominating functions.  This would prove `radius G + 1 ≤ romanDominationNumber G`, as defined by the minimum of the weights. A critical step is ensuring the inequality's generality regarding arbitrary Roman dominating functions.\\n\\nIf this inequality holds, it follows from the definition of the minimum weight. A counterexample must be constructed to disprove the claim that it holds for all functions, not just minimal ones. The current hypothesis considers the implication for functions of increased weight.\\n\\n\\n\"\r\n\"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:27:25+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:27:26+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 0.\\nOutput:\\nChecking ALL Roman dominating functions on all graphs in Atlas...\\r\\n\\n\"\r\n[\r\n  {\r\n    \"name\": \"run_command\",\r\n    \"args\": {\r\n      \"CommandLine\": \"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -u -c '\\\\nimport networkx as nx\\\\nimport itertools\\\\n\\\\natlas = nx.graph_atlas_g()\\\\n\\\\nchecked = 0\\\\nfor G in atlas:\\\\n    n = G.number_of_nodes()\\\\n    if n < 2 or not nx.is_connected(G): continue\\\\n    rad = nx.radius(G)\\\\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\\\\n    for f_tuple in itertools.product([0, 1, 2], repeat=n):\\\\n        f = dict(zip(nodes, f_tuple))\\\\n        valid = True\\\\n        for v in nodes:\\\\n            if f[v] == 0:\\\\n                if not any(f[u] == 2 for u in G.neighbors(v)):\\\\n                    valid = False\\\\n                    break\\\\n   